AHS-007 — Service Model

Document ID: AHS-007
Title: Service Model
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

Architectural Views: Logical, Process

1. Purpose

This specification defines the canonical service model of the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The service model specifies how Atlas components expose capabilities, consume capabilities provided by other components, and collaborate to perform engineering cognition. It establishes standardized service contracts that enable interoperability, modularity, and independent evolution.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Service types
Service responsibilities
Service contracts
Service lifecycle
Service interaction patterns
Service dependencies

It does not define communication protocols, APIs, or implementation technologies.

3. Canonical Definition

An Atlas Service is a well-defined functional capability exposed by an Atlas component through a standardized contract, enabling other components to request engineering operations without knowledge of the underlying implementation.

A service represents what a component provides, not how it is implemented.

4. Service Principles

Every Atlas service SHALL:

Expose a single business or cognitive capability.
Be implementation independent.
Be discoverable.
Have a well-defined contract.
Preserve engineering context.
Support traceability.
Support versioning.
Be independently testable.
5. Canonical Service Categories
5.1 Cognitive Services

Implement engineering cognition.

Examples:

Observation Service
Evidence Validation Service
Understanding Service
Memory Service
Knowledge Service
Reasoning Service
Capability Service
Decision Support Service
5.2 Governance Services

Provide governance across all cognitive activities.

Examples:

Context Service
Intent Service
Constraint Service
Confidence Service
Traceability Service
Explainability Service
5.3 Platform Services

Provide operational support.

Examples:

Workflow Service
Event Service
Configuration Service
Identity Service
Audit Service
Monitoring Service
Notification Service
5.4 Integration Services

Interface with external systems.

Examples:

Import Service
Export Service
CAD Integration Service
BIM Integration Service
PLM Integration Service
Sensor Integration Service
6. Service Contract

Every Atlas service SHALL define:

Identity

Unique service identifier.

Purpose

Clearly defined responsibility.

Inputs

Engineering artifacts or requests consumed by the service.

Outputs

Engineering artifacts or responses produced by the service.

Preconditions

Conditions that SHALL exist before execution.

Postconditions

Conditions guaranteed after successful execution.

Dependencies

Required services or components.

Failure Conditions

Defined failure scenarios and expected responses.

Version

Service contract version.

7. Service Interaction Patterns

Atlas recognizes the following interaction patterns.

Request–Response

Used when an immediate result is required.

Examples:

Evidence validation
Reasoning request
Capability execution
Event-Driven

Used when services react to changes.

Examples:

Observation received
Knowledge updated
Context changed
Pipeline

Used for sequential engineering cognition.

Observation
      │
Evidence
      │
Understanding
      │
Knowledge
      │
Reasoning
      │
Capability
      │
Decision Support
Publish–Subscribe

Used for system-wide notifications.

Examples:

Memory updated
Constraint modified
Audit event generated
8. Service Lifecycle

Every service SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Defined
     │
Implemented
     │
Registered
     │
Available
     │
Invoked
     │
Executing
     │
Completed
     │
Deprecated
     │
Retired
9. Service Discovery

Services SHALL be discoverable through a canonical service registry.

The registry SHALL provide:

Service identity
Service version
Service category
Supported operations
Dependencies
Availability status
10. Service Composition

Services MAY be composed into higher-level workflows.

Example:

Observation Service
        │
Evidence Service
        │
Understanding Service
        │
Reasoning Service
        │
Capability Service
        │
Decision Support Service

Composed services SHALL preserve the responsibilities of their constituent services.

11. Quality Attributes

Every service SHALL be evaluated against:

Attribute	Description
Availability	Service can be invoked when required
Reliability	Produces consistent results
Discoverability	Can be located by consumers
Reusability	Applicable across workflows
Interoperability	Works with other services
Traceability	Maintains provenance
Explainability	Provides rationale where applicable
Scalability	Handles increasing demand
12. Conformance

A service conforms to this specification if it:

Exposes a documented service contract.
Implements a single defined capability.
Preserves ACC semantics.
Supports required quality attributes.
Complies with AHS architectural principles.
13. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

New service categories.
Distributed service orchestration.
Agent-to-agent service contracts.
Semantic service discovery.
Policy-based service composition.

All extensions SHALL remain consistent with the canonical service model.